#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "51c289e1-f118-5444-bf1d-c7e8262c549f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4816" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S$.\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So\n\n$$\nS = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}\n$$\n\nis spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$.\n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S$.\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$, that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119596 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/id #uuid "f9601132-0450-51d2-8559-e83120f5fa2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6237" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$." :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{5}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$. So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$, that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119597 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/id #uuid "5bdbcb40-c8d4-5191-bbcd-1b066e660f68" :question/math-academy-id "e-6257" :question/problem "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$. If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$. So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + x_{3} = 2; - x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} =-2; - 3x_{1} - 2x_{3} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So, $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$.\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5$, $x_2=0$, and $x_3=7$, meaning that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 + x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b36a1dbc-6cc8-55ed-ab7e-397d0f7d8edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118432} {:answer/id #uuid "c29431db-4048-5866-861b-9bb93e31aedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118433} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5e10a4-7f42-5795-8426-3da1506c38e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118434} {:answer/id #uuid "4da800e5-361e-5391-9977-3e379a131f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118435} {:answer/id #uuid "56021b3a-45a6-5587-9b96-a7b61485e1cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5e10a4-7f42-5795-8426-3da1506c38e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118434} :answer-field/id #uuid "3869a980-149c-5358-a935-50e4110701b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118431}] :question/id #uuid "6fe4864a-ed27-50fc-8816-ad1ec4b0dfc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35694" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, $a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$, and $a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a basis $B$ of $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "907119ce-2be5-521e-81b4-787f5c0817a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119182} {:answer/id #uuid "a117133d-45e5-5cb5-bcd4-cb965216c74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is not a basis of $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, a_{4}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119183} {:answer/id #uuid "c0faaa5e-dee7-5a7a-9eac-6a2e0d33cf23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119184} {:answer/id #uuid "247f4737-a0d6-5b95-a5f5-0f11b9b956b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119185} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2cbe11-6b54-551f-85a5-0e02ea9938a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "907119ce-2be5-521e-81b4-787f5c0817a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119182} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1493e6a-ede7-511e-a52f-767a4938be58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119181}] :question/id #uuid "1c72b3a4-9e18-57da-90d5-8d38d6a9dad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75588" :question/problem "The vectors $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, $a_{3}$, and $a_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B = {a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is a basis of $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, a_{4}}$. If that is the case, find $x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}$, where $a_{4} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3}$.\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, a_{4} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
